WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.690
A ministry wants every school's

2
00:00:02.690 --> 00:00:04.250
daily attendance: twenty

3
00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:06.490
thousand schools, many on 2G.

4
00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:09.620
Sent as JSON, that's hours

5
00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:10.980
of airtime every day.

6
00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:12.990
Is there a better way?

7
00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:17.070
Today: designing schemas and choosing

8
00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:19.230
formats for data that moves.

9
00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:23.620
A schema is the shape of your data: its

10
00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:26.400
tables, columns, types and rules.

11
00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:29.850
Apps want it normalised, each fact

12
00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:31.950
stored once, for safe updates.

13
00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:35.500
Reports want it built for reading: a star.

14
00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:39.760
In a star schema, a fact table holds the

15
00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.920
events, like one row per pupil per day.

16
00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:45.930
Dimension tables describe them: the

17
00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:47.890
school, the class, the date.

18
00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:51.180
Reports join and summarise it quickly.

19
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:53.440
Then the format.

20
00:00:54.310 --> 00:00:58.110
CSV and JSON store row by row, and

21
00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:00.950
JSON repeats every column name, so

22
00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:02.750
it's more than twice the size.

23
00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:06.210
Column formats, like Parquet, store

24
00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:08.550
each column together, so repeated

25
00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:10.730
values squeeze to almost nothing.

26
00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:13.680
A school's term of attendance:

27
00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:16.180
twenty-five thousand records.

28
00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:20.210
As JSON over 2G, nearly five minutes.

29
00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:23.440
Compressed, about thirteen seconds.

30
00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:26.190
By column and compressed,

31
00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:28.090
about six kilobytes.

32
00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:29.790
Quick check!

33
00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.280
Why is JSON bigger than CSV

34
00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:34.560
for the same table?

35
00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:37.280
It repeats every column

36
00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:38.700
name in every record.

37
00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:42.270
CSV says the names once, in the header.

38
00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:45.600
Data arriving from outside

39
00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:47.420
must match your schema.

40
00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:50.360
Parse each value to its type, and

41
00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:52.500
count what doesn't fit, instead of

42
00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:53.760
crashing or guessing.

43
00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:56.900
In the app, choose how much

44
00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:58.880
data, the format and the

45
00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:01.700
network, and compress it or not.

46
00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:04.520
Find the cheapest way to send a

47
00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:06.400
school's term over 2G.

48
00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:08.370
Your turn.

49
00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:11.390
A report needs the average attendance

50
00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:13.710
per class over a whole year.

51
00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.500
Which format reads the least data?

52
00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:20.290
A column format: it reads only

53
00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:21.390
the columns it needs.

54
00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:24.570
Remember: normalise for apps,

55
00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:26.550
and use a star for reports.

56
00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:30.630
Formats change the cost: JSON repeats

57
00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:33.530
names, and columns squeeze out repeats.

58
00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:36.200
And check every row against

59
00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:37.660
the schema as it arrives.

60
00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:41.220
Try it tomorrow: save the same

61
00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:44.580
small table as CSV and as JSON,

62
00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:46.640
and compare the file sizes.

63
00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:48.720
Then compress both.

64
00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:51.170
Which wins, and why?
